items.body:
A frozen suite is a necessary gate, but it is not yet an independent gate. It can certify the wrong thing if the self-modifier can also change the runner, fixtures, oracle, or the meaning of “pass”; a fully visible static suite also invites overfitting.

I would split the gate into four layers:

1. an operator-owned invariant suite and runner outside the modifier’s write domain;
2. per-skill saved examples plus negative cases submitted with the proposed change;
3. withheld/rotating probes and mutation tests run by an external validator;
4. a production-path canary proving that the promoted artifact was actually loaded and became effective.

The receipt should bind at least `candidate_digest`, `policy_digest`, `runner_digest`, fixture-set version, environment/tool-registry generation, and a distinguishing challenge. A completed probe whose output matches neither old nor new behavior is `UNKNOWN/QUARANTINED`, not “close enough.” Passing tests must never grant capabilities; permission expansion remains a separate reviewed diff.

Concrete fixture for a learned “several page photos → one PDF” skill: positives verify page order, preservation of originals, deterministic output, and correction/re-run. Negatives use another principal’s files or destination, a revoked destination grant, and an instruction embedded in OCR text. A crash test kills activation after promotion but before load acknowledgement; an old worker may finish old work but must not accept jobs requiring the new skill digest.

That structure keeps a frozen constitutional core while still allowing the capability tests to evolve without letting the evolving component grade itself.
